    <section class="section" id="tensorflow-keras-basics">
      <span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span>
      <h2>TensorFlow and Keras Ecosystem</h2>
      <p>TensorFlow is Google's open-source machine learning framework providing low-level operations for building neural networks. Keras is its high-level API that simplifies model building with an intuitive, layer-based interface. Starting with TensorFlow 2.0, Keras became the standard API, making it the primary way most practitioners build deep learning models.</p>

      <p>Keras follows a simple philosophy: define layers, stack them, compile with a loss function and optimizer, then train. This abstraction handles backpropagation and gradient updates automatically. The framework runs on CPUs, GPUs, and TPUs, with automatic hardware acceleration. Understanding Keras' design patterns is essential for building reproducible, scalable deep learning systems.</p>

      <div class="key-concept">
        <strong>Key Concept:</strong> Keras excels at rapid prototyping and experimentation. Its layer-based architecture mirrors how neural networks are typically described in research papers, making translation from theory to code straightforward and intuitive.
      </div>

      <h3>Sequential API: Simple Model Definition</h3>
      <p>The Sequential API provides the simplest way to build linear stack of layers. This works perfectly for most standard architectures. You specify layers in order, and Keras automatically connects inputs and outputs.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">tensorflow</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">tf</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="va">tensorflow</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">keras</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="va">tensorflow.keras</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">layers</span>

<span class="cm"># Sequential model: simple linear stack</span>
<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">Sequential</span>([
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">128</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>, <span class="va">input_shape</span><span class="op">=</span>(<span class="nm">784</span>,)),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dropout</span>(<span class="nm">0.2</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">64</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dropout</span>(<span class="nm">0.2</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'softmax'</span>)
])

<span class="cm"># Or add layers dynamically</span>
<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">Sequential</span>()
<span class="va">model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">add</span>(<span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">128</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>, <span class="va">input_dim</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">784</span>))
<span class="va">model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">add</span>(<span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'softmax'</span>))</span></code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="layers-and-activations">
      <span class="section-label">Core Components</span>
      <h2>Layers and Activation Functions</h2>
      <p>Layers are the fundamental building blocks of neural networks. Each layer contains neurons and connections with trainable weights and biases. Dense (fully-connected) layers are the simplest; specialized layers like Conv2D, LSTM, and Embedding serve specific purposes. Activation functions introduce non-linearity, enabling networks to learn complex patterns.</p>

      <h3>Dense Layers and Common Activations</h3>
      <p>Dense layers connect every neuron to every neuron in the next layer. This creates the most general-purpose connections. Activation functions determine the output behavior. ReLU is preferred for hidden layers, while softmax is standard for multi-class classification output.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Dense layer with weight initialization</span>
<span class="va">layer</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(
    <span class="nm">64</span>,  <span class="cm"># output units</span>
    <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>,
    <span class="va">kernel_initializer</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'he_normal'</span>,
    <span class="va">bias_initializer</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'zeros'</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Common activation functions</span>
<span class="cm"># ReLU (Rectified Linear Unit): max(0, x) - non-linear, sparse</span>
<span class="va">relu</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">64</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>)

<span class="cm"># Leaky ReLU: allows small negative values</span>
<span class="va">leaky</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">LeakyReLU</span>(<span class="va">alpha</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.1</span>)

<span class="cm"># Sigmoid: outputs in (0, 1), used for binary classification</span>
<span class="va">sigmoid</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'sigmoid'</span>)

<span class="cm"># Softmax: probability distribution over classes</span>
<span class="va">softmax</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'softmax'</span>)</span></code></pre>

      <h3>Regularization Layers</h3>
      <p>Dropout randomly sets neurons to zero during training, forcing the network to learn robust features. Batch Normalization standardizes layer inputs to accelerate training and improve generalization. L1/L2 regularization penalizes large weights, reducing overfitting.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Dropout: randomly deactivate neurons</span>
<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">Sequential</span>([
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">128</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dropout</span>(<span class="nm">0.3</span>),  <span class="cm"># 30% dropout</span>
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'softmax'</span>)
])

<span class="cm"># Batch Normalization: standardize inputs to layer</span>
<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">Sequential</span>([
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">128</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">BatchNormalization</span>(),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'softmax'</span>)
])

<span class="cm"># L2 regularization: penalize large weights</span>
<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">Sequential</span>([
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(
        <span class="nm">128</span>,
        <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>,
        <span class="va">kernel_regularizer</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">regularizers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">l2</span>(<span class="nm">0.01</span>)
    ),
    <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'softmax'</span>)
])</span></code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="model-compilation-training">
      <span class="section-label">Model Workflow</span>
      <h2>Compilation and Training</h2>
      <p>Compiling a model specifies the loss function, optimizer, and metrics. The loss function guides learning toward the correct target. The optimizer determines how weights are updated (SGD, Adam, RMSprop). Metrics track performance during training. Training iterates through data in batches, computing gradients and updating weights.</p>

      <h3>Compile Configuration</h3>
      <p>The compile step is crucial. Choosing the right loss function depends on your task: categorical_crossentropy for multi-class classification, binary_crossentropy for binary classification, and MSE for regression. Adam is usually a good default optimizer.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Compile for classification</span>
<span class="va">model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">compile</span>(
    <span class="va">loss</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'categorical_crossentropy'</span>,
    <span class="va">optimizer</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'adam'</span>,
    <span class="va">metrics</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">'accuracy'</span>, <span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">metrics</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Precision</span>()]
)

<span class="cm"># Compile with custom optimizer</span>
<span class="va">model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">compile</span>(
    <span class="va">loss</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'mse'</span>,
    <span class="va">optimizer</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">optimizers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Adam</span>(<span class="va">learning_rate</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.001</span>),
    <span class="va">metrics</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">'mae'</span>]  <span class="cm"># mean absolute error</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Binary classification</span>
<span class="va">model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">compile</span>(
    <span class="va">loss</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'binary_crossentropy'</span>,
    <span class="va">optimizer</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'adam'</span>,
    <span class="va">metrics</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">'accuracy'</span>, <span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">metrics</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">AUC</span>()]
)</span></code></pre>

      <h3>Training with fit()</h3>
      <p>The fit() method trains the model. You provide training data, number of epochs, batch size, and validation data. Keras automatically handles batching, shuffling, and gradient updates. Using a validation set helps detect overfitting. Early stopping can halt training when validation loss plateaus.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Basic training</span>
<span class="va">history</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">fit</span>(
    <span class="va">x_train</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>,
    <span class="va">epochs</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">50</span>,
    <span class="va">batch_size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">32</span>,
    <span class="va">validation_split</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.2</span>  <span class="cm"># 20% validation</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Training with early stopping and callbacks</span>
<span class="va">early_stop</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">callbacks</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">EarlyStopping</span>(
    <span class="va">monitor</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'val_loss'</span>,
    <span class="va">patience</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">10</span>,  <span class="cm"># stop if no improvement for 10 epochs</span>
    <span class="va">restore_best_weights</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">True</span>
)

<span class="va">history</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">fit</span>(
    <span class="va">x_train</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>,
    <span class="va">epochs</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">100</span>,
    <span class="va">batch_size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">32</span>,
    <span class="va">validation_data</span><span class="op">=</span>(<span class="va">x_val</span>, <span class="va">y_val</span>),
    <span class="va">callbacks</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="va">early_stop</span>],
    <span class="va">verbose</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1</span>
)</span></code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="functional-api">
      <span class="section-label">Advanced Architecture</span>
      <h2>Functional API for Complex Models</h2>
      <p>The Functional API enables building models with non-sequential topologies: multiple inputs, multiple outputs, shared layers, and skip connections. While more verbose than Sequential, it provides flexibility needed for advanced architectures like ResNets and multi-task models.</p>

      <h3>Building with the Functional API</h3>
      <p>In the Functional API, you explicitly connect layer outputs to inputs. This approach treats models as graphs of layers, where data flows from inputs through transformations to outputs.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Multi-input, single-output model</span>
<span class="va">input_a</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">Input</span>(<span class="va">shape</span><span class="op">=</span>(<span class="nm">32</span>,))
<span class="va">input_b</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">Input</span>(<span class="va">shape</span><span class="op">=</span>(<span class="nm">64</span>,))

<span class="va">x</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">64</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>)(<span class="va">input_a</span>)
<span class="va">y</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">64</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>)(<span class="va">input_b</span>)

<span class="va">concatenated</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Concatenate</span>()([<span class="va">x</span>, <span class="va">y</span>])
<span class="va">z</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">32</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>)(<span class="va">concatenated</span>)
<span class="va">output</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'softmax'</span>)(<span class="va">z</span>)

<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Model</span>(<span class="va">inputs</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="va">input_a</span>, <span class="va">input_b</span>], <span class="va">outputs</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">output</span>)

<span class="cm"># Multi-output model</span>
<span class="va">main_input</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">Input</span>(<span class="va">shape</span><span class="op">=</span>(<span class="nm">100</span>,))
<span class="va">x</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">64</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'relu'</span>)(<span class="va">main_input</span>)

<span class="va">classification_output</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="va">activation</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'softmax'</span>, <span class="va">name</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'class'</span>)(<span class="va">x</span>)
<span class="va">regression_output</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">layers</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Dense</span>(<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="va">name</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'value'</span>)(<span class="va">x</span>)

<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">Model</span>(<span class="va">inputs</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">main_input</span>, <span class="va">outputs</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="va">classification_output</span>, <span class="va">regression_output</span>])</span></code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="evaluation-deployment">
      <span class="section-label">Evaluation and Deployment</span>
      <h2>Model Evaluation and Saving</h2>
      <p>After training, evaluate your model on a held-out test set. Keras provides easy ways to visualize training history, inspect predictions, and save models for later deployment. Models can be saved in multiple formats suitable for serving.</p>

      <h3>Evaluation and Predictions</h3>
      <p>Use evaluate() to get loss and metrics on test data. Use predict() to generate predictions on new data. Visualizing training history helps identify overfitting and validate training dynamics.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Evaluate on test set</span>
<span class="va">test_loss</span>, <span class="va">test_accuracy</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">evaluate</span>(<span class="va">x_test</span>, <span class="va">y_test</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Test accuracy: {test_accuracy:.4f}"</span>)

<span class="cm"># Generate predictions</span>
<span class="va">predictions</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">predict</span>(<span class="va">x_test</span>)
<span class="va">class_predictions</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">np</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">argmax</span>(<span class="va">predictions</span>, <span class="va">axis</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1</span>)

<span class="cm"># Visualize training history</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">matplotlib</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">pyplot</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="va">plt</span>

<span class="va">plt</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">plot</span>(<span class="va">history</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">history</span>[<span class="st">'loss'</span>], <span class="va">label</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'Training Loss'</span>)
<span class="va">plt</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">plot</span>(<span class="va">history</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">history</span>[<span class="st">'val_loss'</span>], <span class="va">label</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'Validation Loss'</span>)
<span class="va">plt</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">xlabel</span>(<span class="st">'Epoch'</span>)
<span class="va">plt</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">ylabel</span>(<span class="st">'Loss'</span>)
<span class="va">plt</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">legend</span>()
<span class="va">plt</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">show</span>()</span></code></pre>

      <h3>Saving and Loading Models</h3>
      <p>Keras supports multiple formats for saving models. The SavedModel format is recommended for deployment and is compatible with TensorFlow Serving. H5 format is legacy but still widely used. Always save checkpoints during training in case training is interrupted.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Save entire model (architecture + weights)</span>
<span class="va">model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">save</span>(<span class="st">'my_model.keras'</span>)  <span class="cm"># Keras format (recommended)</span>
<span class="va">model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">save</span>(<span class="st">'my_model.h5'</span>)      <span class="cm"># HDF5 format (legacy)</span>

<span class="cm"># Load model</span>
<span class="va">loaded_model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">models</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">load_model</span>(<span class="st">'my_model.keras'</span>)

<span class="cm"># Save weights only</span>
<span class="va">model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">save_weights</span>(<span class="st">'weights.h5'</span>)

<span class="cm"># Checkpoint during training</span>
<span class="va">checkpoint</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">callbacks</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">ModelCheckpoint</span>(
    <span class="st">'best_model.keras'</span>,
    <span class="va">monitor</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'val_loss'</span>,
    <span class="va">save_best_only</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">True</span>
)

<span class="va">model</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">fit</span>(<span class="va">x_train</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>, <span class="va">callbacks</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="va">checkpoint</span>])</span></code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="exercises">
      <span class="section-label">Practice</span>
      <h2>Exercises and Challenges</h2>

      <h3>Exercise 1: MNIST Classification</h3>
      <p>Build and train a neural network on the MNIST digit classification dataset. Implement dropout, batch normalization, and early stopping. Evaluate on test set and visualize misclassifications.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Load MNIST</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="va">keras.datasets</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">mnist</span>

<span class="va">x_train</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">mnist</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">load_data</span>()[<span class="nm">0</span>]
<span class="va">x_test</span>, <span class="va">y_test</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">mnist</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">load_data</span>()[<span class="nm">1</span>]

<span class="cm"># Normalize and reshape</span>
<span class="va">x_train</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">x_train</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">reshape</span>(<span class="op">-</span><span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">784</span>)<span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">astype</span>(<span class="st">'float32'</span>) <span class="op">/</span> <span class="nm">255</span>
<span class="va">x_test</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">x_test</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">reshape</span>(<span class="op">-</span><span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">784</span>)<span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">astype</span>(<span class="st">'float32'</span>) <span class="op">/</span> <span class="nm">255</span>

<span class="cm"># One-hot encode labels</span>
<span class="va">y_train</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">utils</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">to_categorical</span>(<span class="va">y_train</span>, <span class="nm">10</span>)
<span class="va">y_test</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">keras</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">utils</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">to_categorical</span>(<span class="va">y_test</span>, <span class="nm">10</span>)

<span class="cm"># Build and train model (your code)</span></code></pre>

      <h3>Exercise 2: Multi-Output Model</h3>
      <p>Create a model that predicts both the age and income from demographic features using the Functional API. Implement separate output layers with appropriate loss functions.</p>

