    <section class="section" id="key-takeaways">
      <span class="section-label">Summary</span>
      <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>

      <div class="takeaway-grid">
        <div class="takeaway-card">
          <h4>Sequential API</h4>
          <p>Best for simple, linear architectures. Easy to define, fast to prototype, and perfect for most standard neural networks.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="takeaway-card">
          <h4>Functional API</h4>
          <p>Needed for complex models with multiple inputs, outputs, or non-linear connections. More flexible than Sequential but more verbose.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="takeaway-card">
          <h4>Activation Functions</h4>
          <p>ReLU for hidden layers, softmax for multi-class output, sigmoid for binary. Choose activation based on your task.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="takeaway-card">
          <h4>Regularization</h4>
          <p>Dropout and batch normalization reduce overfitting. L1/L2 regularization penalizes large weights. Use together for best results.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="takeaway-card">
          <h4>Training Strategy</h4>
          <p>Use validation data and early stopping. Monitor training curves for overfitting. Save checkpoints during training.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="takeaway-card">
          <h4>Model Persistence</h4>
          <p>Save complete models with weights and architecture for production. Use ModelCheckpoint to save best weights during training.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

